Disclaimer:
~~~~~~~~~~
    Originally, v1.3 was written by PMK, aka Peter Klein. Since Peter has
decided to go the IBM route, I'll be attempting to "take on" the continued
development of the files he has authored in the past.

    Also, a v2.1 by StoneHead is floating around as well, but that version
was programmed in "C", so this ARexx version won't run "quite" as fast, but
it DOES work flawlessly, and DOES allow the code to be modified by the SysOp
who chooses to use it! This is another utility that I still want to see the
"FP" label retained on! :-) This version should run equally well on BOTH
CNet, v3.05c and CNet, v4.11+ (Tested under v4.12d!).

Overview:
~~~~~~~~
   FPDD (FP Download Descriptions) allows the user to download a Description
List of the files he/she is about to download. It offers 2 different kinds
of Description Lists.

1. Standard  (A formatted, readable output!)

2. FILES.BBS (This is useful for adopting the files you have downloaded
              onto your own BBS, or when uploading to other CNet BBS's!)

   Other features include: formatted output, support for FILES.BBS type of
description, grabs descriptions from CD-roms and multiple UDBASEx: assigns,
and grabs the filenotes, if no CNet short description is available.

How to Install?
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1. Copy the "FPDD/" directory into your "FPFiles/" directory if you have one,
   or else copy the "FPFiles/FPDD/" directory anywhere on your system.

   NOTE: All Dotoran released "FP" files will use the "FPFiles/" directory
         as their storage location to make working with these files as easy
         as possible. It is HIGHLY recommended that you do the same...

2. Load your BBSMENU file into an editor and go to Menu # 24; Abort/Logoff
   prompt before file transfer, then add the following line:

        Descriptions    |   {#0<path>FPFiles/FPDD/FPDD 0}
                            ^                         ^
                        Control-Q                   SPACE

   NOTE1: Replace <path> with the one you used in Step # 1 above.

   NOTE2: If you'd like NOT to support the ability to ASK the user what
          format they'd like the description file created in, you may
          specify a second argument in the above line:

            {#0<path>FPFiles/FPDD/FPDD 0 0} - Always create as FILES.BBS.
            ^                         ^ ^
            {#0<path>FPFiles/FPDD/FPDD 0 1} - Always create as Standard.
            ^                         ^ ^
        Control-Q's                 SPACES!

    PROGRAMMERS: The first "0" specifies that FPDD is being used by a
                 REMOTE user. A "1" is specified as the 1st argument in
                 Step 4 below to specify LOCAL use of the FPDD file.

3. Save your BBSMENU, then load BBSTEXT, and go to line 615. You'll need
   to CHANGE this line, so the following appears ALL ON ONE LINE(it's been
   broken down into multiple lines here for readability ONLY):

        \n2[A]bort, [L]ogoff Upon Completion{T1107358 #85}{JE 1+}
        {T1107358 #117}{JE 1+}, [D]ownload Desc.{1} [ENTER=Start]>

   NOTE: Remember, place the above ALL ON ONE LINE. There is NO SPACE
         used to connect the two portions together. Replace the one \
         with a Control-Y, and the five {'s with Control-Q characters.

   PROGRAMMERS: The MCI's used here check the command last used, and if
                it's seen to be either a "U" or a "u", then the user will
                NOT be shown the " ,[D]ownload Desc." text.

4. To also use the FPDD with Local Downloads, add the following to the
   beginning of line 579. Again, the following appears ON ONE LINE and
   should come BEFORE what you presently have on line 579:

        \n1Create Description file [No]? \?0{T70 #0}{JE 1+}
        {#0<path>FPFiles/FPDD/FPDD 1}\n1{1}

   NOTE: Replace <path> with what you used in Step # 1, and remember that
         all of what you see above appears ON ONE LINE, and should be
         placed BEFORE the "Copy to Path:" text that appears on line 579.
         There are three \'s and four {'s that need to be changed to the
         appropriate Control-Y / Control-Q characters in this line.

   NOTE2: You could also specify the 2nd argument within the above command,
          too, if you KNOW you'd always be using one format or another,
          while doing local DOWNLOADS.

5. That's It! Save your BBSTEXT file, then reload both files and you're
   ready to start using FPDownloadDescriptions!
                                                                - Dotoran
